WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:07.200
Okay, so with that we have learned how to insert data to our tables.

00:07.240 --> 00:09.400
Now let's say that I don't have something new.

00:09.400 --> 00:12.120
I don't have any rows to be added to my table.

00:12.160 --> 00:13.640
But I have an update.

00:13.640 --> 00:18.040
I would like to go and change the content of the already existing rows.

00:18.040 --> 00:19.040
So what we can do?

00:19.040 --> 00:25.560
We can use the command updates in order to change the content of already existing rows.

00:25.560 --> 00:31.920
So again my friends insert going to go and insert completely new rows, but update can go and change

00:31.920 --> 00:34.120
the data of already existing row.

00:34.200 --> 00:36.720
Now let's have a look quickly to the syntax of the updates.

00:36.720 --> 00:40.400
It starts with the keyword updates and then we have to specify the table name.

00:40.400 --> 00:45.960
And after that we're going to use it in order to specify what are the new values for the columns.

00:45.960 --> 00:49.760
So you have to write down for each column that you want to update a new value.

00:49.760 --> 00:52.120
And you separate the columns of course using a comma.

00:52.160 --> 00:55.720
Now after that we have to specify as well a where condition.

00:55.720 --> 00:58.800
So it's like the queries you say where and then you write a condition.

00:58.800 --> 01:03.360
And if you don't do that and you don't use the Where clause, what can happen?

01:03.360 --> 01:07.320
You will end up updating all the rows inside your table.

01:07.320 --> 01:09.440
So that's why we need always the where clause.

01:09.480 --> 01:11.200
All right so that's all about the syntax.

01:11.200 --> 01:14.040
Let's go back to SQL in order to update our data.

01:14.080 --> 01:14.320
Okay.

01:14.360 --> 01:15.560
So let's have the following task.

01:15.560 --> 01:19.120
And it says change the score of customer 6 to 0.

01:19.120 --> 01:23.400
So that means we have to go and modify the data of the customer ID equal to six.

01:23.400 --> 01:26.080
So now first I would like to go and have a look to our data.

01:26.080 --> 01:28.760
So select star from customers.

01:28.800 --> 01:31.720
And now the task is targeting this customer over here.

01:31.720 --> 01:34.600
And we would like to replace the null to zero.

01:34.600 --> 01:37.800
Now how can go and update this information inside a table.

01:37.800 --> 01:39.760
We can use the update command.

01:39.760 --> 01:42.880
So what we're going to do we're going to start writing update.

01:43.080 --> 01:45.640
And after that we have to specify the table name.

01:45.640 --> 01:48.280
So what we are updating we are updating the customers.

01:48.280 --> 01:53.160
And then we're going to tell the database to set the value of the score to zero.

01:53.160 --> 01:56.720
So we'd like to update and change the value from null to zero.

01:56.720 --> 01:58.560
And now here comes something very risky.

01:58.600 --> 02:00.200
Don't execute this query yet.

02:00.240 --> 02:01.960
If you do that what's going to happen?

02:01.960 --> 02:08.080
The database is going to go to the table customers and replace all those values of all customers to

02:08.120 --> 02:08.560
zero.

02:08.560 --> 02:10.480
So it can go and update the whole table.

02:10.480 --> 02:12.160
And this is, of course, very risky.

02:12.200 --> 02:18.000
That's why in the update command we have to give a where condition a filter in order to target only

02:18.000 --> 02:21.360
specific row or the rows that you want really to motivate.

02:21.360 --> 02:23.640
In this case we want to change only one row.

02:23.680 --> 02:29.520
So what we have to do is to go and specify the where condition like we have done in the Select query.

02:29.560 --> 02:30.600
Nothing new right?

02:30.600 --> 02:34.640
So we're going to say where the customer ID is equal to six.

02:34.640 --> 02:37.360
And with that SQL will not go and update everything.

02:37.400 --> 02:40.200
First it's going to filter the data and then update.

02:40.240 --> 02:44.680
And now before I execute, just to make sure I go and check which data can be affected.

02:44.680 --> 02:45.400
So it's very simple.

02:45.400 --> 02:48.200
You go and select star from table customers.

02:48.200 --> 02:52.560
And then I go and take the exact where and put it in my query.

02:52.560 --> 02:55.200
And then I select the whole thing and execute.

02:55.200 --> 03:00.720
And now if this query gives me the data that should be modified, then I'm doing the update command

03:00.720 --> 03:01.320
correctly.

03:01.320 --> 03:04.360
And in this case we are targeting only one customer.

03:04.400 --> 03:05.920
This is the customer number six.

03:05.920 --> 03:08.760
And with that I feel really confident with my update.

03:08.760 --> 03:13.400
So what we can do, since I'm going to use this later, I'm going to put the whole thing in a command.

03:13.400 --> 03:16.640
And if I execute now, only the update is update can be executed.

03:16.640 --> 03:18.560
So let's go and do that now.

03:18.560 --> 03:20.160
Very important to check the message.

03:20.160 --> 03:25.960
You can see one row is affected which is really good because if I see here ten rows is affected.

03:25.960 --> 03:28.120
That means everything is updated.

03:28.160 --> 03:31.120
Now let's go and check the data.

03:31.160 --> 03:33.840
I'm going to go and remove the where here and check the whole table.

03:33.880 --> 03:36.040
Now you can see we still have the old scores.

03:36.120 --> 03:39.560
Only Anna has now scored zero instead of null.

03:39.560 --> 03:41.520
So this is how I usually update the data.

03:41.520 --> 03:43.320
You have to do it very carefully.

03:43.320 --> 03:45.200
Now let's move to another task.

03:45.200 --> 03:51.160
It's gonna say change the score of the customer number 10 to 0 and update the country to UK.

03:51.240 --> 03:54.200
So now this time we are targeting the user number ten.

03:54.240 --> 03:56.880
As you can see, she doesn't have the country and score.

03:56.880 --> 04:01.160
And the task wants us to change the score to zero and the country to UK.

04:01.200 --> 04:02.040
So now how we can do it.

04:02.040 --> 04:05.800
We're going to use the exact same commands but with different condition.

04:05.800 --> 04:10.200
So the ID this time is equal to ten and the score is to zero.

04:10.200 --> 04:12.280
But now we have to change as well the country.

04:12.320 --> 04:17.720
Now if you want to do multiple updates, you're going to have here a comma after the score and the new

04:17.720 --> 04:20.330
line and let's say country Three equal.

04:20.370 --> 04:22.050
And then we can add UK.

04:22.170 --> 04:25.730
So select the whole thing and let's go and execute.

04:25.730 --> 04:28.050
So again it is affecting only one row.

04:28.090 --> 04:28.930
This is really good.

04:28.930 --> 04:31.890
And if you go and check the table search for Sara.

04:31.930 --> 04:37.050
You can see in one update we have updated two columns the country and as well the score.

04:37.210 --> 04:39.010
So with that we have solved the task.

04:39.010 --> 04:39.850
It's very simple.

04:39.890 --> 04:46.450
Now moving on to the second task it says update all customers with a null score by setting their score

04:46.450 --> 04:47.410
to zero.

04:47.410 --> 04:50.850
So this time we are not speaking about one specific customer.

04:50.850 --> 04:54.690
We are talking about updating the data for a subset of customers.

04:54.730 --> 04:59.490
So now imagine you have like hundreds of customers and you are making one update command for each customer.

04:59.490 --> 05:01.210
It's going to be really wasting your time.

05:01.210 --> 05:06.450
Now instead of that, we can specify a condition that targets multiple customers.

05:06.490 --> 05:09.450
And we're going to do the update for those customers in one go.

05:09.450 --> 05:10.650
So now let's see how we're going to do it.

05:10.690 --> 05:14.250
We are talking only about replacing the nulls with zero.

05:14.250 --> 05:15.730
So we don't need a country.

05:15.730 --> 05:17.970
So set score equal to zero.

05:17.970 --> 05:20.690
But now we will not be specific for the IDs.

05:20.730 --> 05:22.330
Now we have to make a new condition.

05:22.330 --> 05:25.490
It's going to say like this where score is null.

05:25.810 --> 05:29.250
Now, of course, in the course we have a full dedicated chapter about the nulls.

05:29.250 --> 05:33.770
And here all what you are doing is we are searching for scores that is equal to null.

05:33.770 --> 05:35.010
But we cannot write an equal.

05:35.010 --> 05:36.890
We have to write it like this is null.

05:36.930 --> 05:41.610
Now of course, before we update anything we have to go and test it in a query.

05:41.610 --> 05:44.530
So select star from customers where score is null.

05:44.570 --> 05:45.810
Let's go and execute.

05:45.850 --> 05:49.850
Now as you can see we have two customers where the score is null.

05:49.850 --> 05:53.770
So that means this condition is targeting the subset of customers.

05:53.770 --> 05:57.810
And we're going to do now the updates for multiple rows for this subset.

05:57.810 --> 06:00.290
So that means we can run this query.

06:00.330 --> 06:01.650
Let's go and execute it.

06:01.690 --> 06:04.090
Now you can see two rows are affected.

06:04.090 --> 06:08.490
So that means multiple rows got affected got updated.

06:08.490 --> 06:14.650
So now if you go and query our table customers you can see we don't have any nulls inside the scores.

06:14.690 --> 06:17.210
And we have replaced all the nulls with zero.

06:17.210 --> 06:18.650
And of course you can do the same thing.

06:18.650 --> 06:24.890
You can go and make an update command in order to replace all the nulls in the country to maybe something

06:24.930 --> 06:27.130
unknown or any default value that you want.

06:27.170 --> 06:30.570
So this is how you can update multiple rows in one go.
